## 20. The persona's "today" is the universe date, not the Pacific day of the freeze

- The freeze 2026-02-14T00:00Z is Friday 4:00 pm Pacific, so the design treated Friday as "now" and the prompt said Leonard's Friday 10:12 am note was "this morning". The platform reads the universe date "February 14, 2026" as the persona's today (Saturday), so "this morning" was false (Truthfulness FAIL).
- Compute every relative word (today, this morning, yesterday, last week) against the universe date. Prefer "yesterday" or a weekday name; a record time inside an authority ("as of 4:00 pm Pacific on Friday 13 February") can still name the instant.

## 21. Every pair of rule sentences must agree on every seeded account

- Sunset's note said "Open if … still in force …, including one you have cancelled that runs to a later end date" and "A termination you have asked for counts from the day the vendor confirms it or the service actually stops". Helpshift (confirmed 11 Feb, running to the end of February) satisfied both and got opposite answers (fork F1).
- Before /injection, apply each rule sentence to each account class, especially the boundaries: confirmed-but-running, requested-but-unconfirmed, cancelled with no stop date, moved before or after a bill. Word the rule for its intent ("only takes effect once the vendor confirms it, and the account stays in force until the service actually stops").

## 22. A rule keyed to a date needs that date on every account it touches

- "A paid plan you have cancelled … ended on the day the paid plan stopped" needs a stop date. CodeRabbit and Krisp said only "cancelled 12 Feb", so either row set was defensible (fork F2). Write "closed straight away" or the paid-through date, and check base records for it (CodeRabbit's base expiry mail said the subscription ended 12 Feb).

## 23. A completeness claim is a claim about the whole base

- The tracker said "One issue per account billed to the company in the last 12 months". The base had Linear on an annual plan "we keep during transition", and a census later found Miro, BrowserStack, Cogency Global and others with no issue either (fork F3 plus a coherence finding).
- Don't let a seeded record claim completeness unless a census proves it. Scope the deliverable explicitly instead. Here Leonard's note now says "if you find something we pay for that isn't in the project, leave that one to me", and the descriptions say "accounts I found on the statements".
- Adding the missing accounts was riskier: Linear and BrowserStack are paid on a card (0785) that no record ties to the company, so an "Open company account" row could itself be challenged under the founders' own-card rule.

## 24. Fix the whole class, not the named instances

- The /injection fix moved Cursor and Krisp after Arthur's 22:59Z report but missed CodeRabbit, which sat in the same Slack assignment line. /prompt then failed it.
- When a finding names some accounts, find every sibling in the same base record or rule class and fix them together.

## 25. /injection PASS does not clear /prompt

- /injection checks records against the base; /prompt re-reads the same records for answer forks and cross-service coherence, with four blind deep-recheck passes. Run the fork sweep (every rule × every account, plus untracked base entities) before submitting either.
- Store every platform-reviewed revision before regenerating. The rebuild script overwrote the stored rev2 SQL that /injection had passed; each revision now goes to its own `run_forensics/revN/`.

## 26. Injected Confluence pages never load; check a reported fork against the whole account first

- The platform states that injection SQL can't edit Confluence (Eval 1 Phase 3.2, base-data-v1): the two injected Ops pages will not load at run time, and the Linear project's pointer to them dangles. It stayed an unscored advisory (A3) here only because the solver reads no Confluence record and every rule on the pages is restated elsewhere (labels in Leonard's Slack note; "the later comment stands" is the ordinary newer-source check). The design counts Confluence among its ten injected schemas, so dropping the pages would fail the hardness gate's `services_injected` (need ≥ 10). In the next design, reach ten schemas without Confluence and never put a rule or a fact only on an injected page.
- Red team A's round 11 failed seven accounts (Frame.io, LinkedIn Sales Navigator, Lokalise, Mixpanel, Rive, Smartlook, Statuspage) as bare "cancelled" notes, but each had a later paid-through date on Contract & notice or a paid re-activation on Data & access. Before changing truth for a reported fork, extract every comment on the parent and its four sub-issues in time order, then send the trail back to the reviewer; here it withdrew the finding in round 12.
- Gate mechanics: regenerate each stage's output after the upstream `--paste`, because the paste rewrites the upstream file and an output assembled earlier fails `structure.fresh`. For a doc-only change with byte-identical SQL, rebuild without `--final` and run only the preflight and manifest-binding tail: the forecast key hashes the whole ledger, so every `--final` run adds another pre-registration.

