# dd-2024-release-cost: Opus 5.5 pre-check too easy (rev1), and the Hardness v2 adoption

Recorded: 1 November 2026
Task: dd-2024-release-cost — Simon Walker / Engineering / Long Horizon, Medium, synth-cut (Oct 1, 2025 universe)
Status: rev1 passed /injection and scored /prompt NON-FAIL 3. One unhinted Opus 5.5 pre-check solved it completely and
/hardness returned FAIL, score 1 ("too easy"). The same day the client issued Hardness v2
(`Task_Authoring/references/Docs/13_Hardness_v2_Opus55.md`), which explains the result; the pipeline now carries it
(`references/Hardness_Mastery.md` §13, `tools/hardness_v2_check.py`). One run only: an observation, not a rate.

## 45. A correction the agent passes on the way is no trap

- Every trap was settled on its first read. Ines's contractor_fees amendment (T1) was a reply in her own thread, the
  thread that delegates to the method doc, so reading the thread returned the original and the amendment together. Both
  train-map versions and "the December version stands" came back in one Trello card read (T3). The slot count's
  correction sat in the anchored board sheet (T4). Two cancelled calendar slots showed in one `gcal_list_events` call,
  and archived tickets came with the first `includeArchived` listing.
- Hardness v2 measured the same on 46 tasks: a correction works only "out of the way" (a Slack message withdrawing a
  GitHub approval, a Confluence comment reopening a Linear ticket). In the same channel or thread, Opus 5.5 checks
  stale-vs-newer by default.
- `tools/hardness_v2_check.py` flags it as SAME_PLACE: `same_container`, `first_read` (an anchor's thread or file) or
  `on_delegation_path` (the record that delegates to the original). On rev1 it flags T1, T3 and T4. A literal reading
  of v2 ("the same thread as the original") misses T1: the original is a doc, but the agent reaches it through the thread
  that holds the correction.

## 46. Brute force beats per-record sampling traps

- The run split all 1,425 Done tickets across six subagents and read every comment: 897 `linear_list_comments` calls,
  1,002 calls in all, 11 minutes, $22. Per-ticket pick notes cost money, not accuracy.
- Decoys (the #analytics labels × payroll note, the "~3 slots" deck, the ZM train map, the art-team Confluence page) were
  never on its path. It went from Leonard's message straight to each authority.
- v2 Part 4 agrees: long inventories, routine bulk writes and straight totals pass 6/6. Volume buys horizon calls, never
  failures. Build difficulty from the kind of decision (v2 Part 3), and keep the ≥ 500-call floor as a horizon target.

## 47. Bind a hash to what the portal actually ingested

- The portal's injection report records the SQL body it graded: from the first section comment (`-- slack`) to the
  end, with trailing blank lines stripped (349,107 bytes, sha256 `784bfe2f…`). The local file adds a five-line header
  and two trailing newlines (349,450 bytes, `a38659c0…`). The portal's displayed character count ran 25 below the body
  on both submissions.
- Comparing whole-file hashes produced a false "the SQL changed since grading" alarm. Hash the body the same way the
  portal does before claiming a PASS no longer covers the file; byte counts alone do not prove identity.
- The synth-cut clock is 2025-10-01 07:00 UTC (00:00 PT), not 00:00 UTC: the portal's lint ran at
  `clock 2025-10-01T07:00:00Z`. `11_V3_Changes.md` was corrected; the assignment's `freeze_utc` stays the user's call.

## 48. Hardness v2 in the pipeline, and its four reconciliations

- New mechanism ids A24 (done across services; family `done_gap`) and A25 (completion elsewhere;
  `completion_elsewhere`) cover v2 traps 1 and 9. Trap 8 (wrong inputs to a total) reuses existing correction mechanisms
  on an aggregate slot and is graded as two rows: the corrected input and the final figure.
- v2's "guard row" and "grade that the agent leaves those items alone" become an exact count or scope on the required
  write ("creates records for exactly 17 of the 20 receipts"): the grader fails any row an idle agent passes.
- v2's "put weight 5 on the trap rows" means design the trap onto 5-band work; never raise a weight. DECISION_FLIP flags
  a rule that can flip no weight-5 slot.
- v2's "no mode is treated as Medium" applies only to the advisory geometry (`difficulty_mutation.caps_for`, which used to
  default to Hard). Authoring still refuses a missing mode.
- Every `tools/*.py` edit changes `stage_gate`'s `__gate_policy__` (sha256 of all tools) and stales every pasted gate
  certificate in a task in flight. Batch tool edits, commit them together, and tell any concurrent session to re-paste.

